hadiths[89]{hadithnumber,text}:
"6895","Sahl ibn Hunayf رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ dispatched him as a messenger to the people of Makkah, commanding him to convey salam and three commands, among them prohibition of swearing by other than Allah, then mentioned the rest of the hadith as already passed. > **""You are my messenger to the people of Makkah. Say: Indeed the Messenger of Allah conveys salam to you and commands you with three things: Do not swear by other than Allah.""**"
"6896","Samurah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade swearing by taghuts and by forefathers, commanding swearing by Allah, with the addition that Allah loves to be sworn by, and forbidding the oath of Shaytan. > **""Do not swear by taghuts, nor swear by your forefathers, and swear by Allah, for He loves that you swear by Him. And do not swear by the oath of Shaytan.""**"
"6897","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning one who swears an oath in such form that he is as he said. > **""Whoever swears an oath, he is as he said. If he said: I am a Jew, then he is a Jew. And if he said: I am a Christian, then he is a Christian. And if he said: I am a Magian, then he is a Magian.""**"
"6898","Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه reported that a Jew came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: What an excellent nation your nation is were they not to equate, explaining they say: What Allah wills and what you will; and were they not to associate, explaining they say: By the right of so-and-so and by the life of so-and-so. The Prophet ﷺ corrected the first and commanded swearing only by Allah. > **""How do they equate? Say: Then you will. Whoever must swear, let him not swear except by Allah.""**"
"6899","Abdullah رضي الله عنه, meaning Ibn Masud, said: To swear falsely by Allah is more beloved to me than to swear by other than Him while truthful."
"6900","Abdullah رضي الله عنه said: Do not swear by the oath of Shaytan that one of you says: By the might of Allah. But say as Allah said: Allah, Lord of Might."
"6901","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Prophet ﷺ heard a man swearing by amanah and rebuked him. > **""Are you not the one who swears by amanah?""**"
"6902","Abu Musa رضي الله عنه reported that two men from Hadramawt disputed before the Prophet ﷺ concerning land belonging to one of them, so the Prophet ﷺ set about making one of them swear, whereupon the other cried out that his land would thus go. The Prophet ﷺ warned of looking, purification and painful punishment for unjust seizure by oath, whereupon the other became cautious and returned it. > **""If he severs it by his oath unjustly, he will be among those whom Allah will not look at on the Day of Resurrection, nor purify him, and for him is a painful punishment.""**"
"6903","Adi ibn Umayrah رضي الله عنه reported that a man of Kindah called Imru al-Qays ibn Abis disputed with a man of Hadramawt before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Judgment of proof was upon the Hadrami, but he had no proof, so judgment of oath fell upon Imru al-Qays. The Hadrami said: If you grant him the oath, O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, or by the Lord of the Kabah, my land will go. The Prophet ﷺ warned of meeting Allah angry for severing wealth by a lying oath, and Raja recited: Indeed those who purchase with the covenant of Allah and their oaths a small price [Al Imran: 77]. Imru al-Qays asked what for one who abandons it, was told Paradise, and testified he had abandoned it all for him. > **""Whoever swears a lying oath to sever thereby the wealth of anyone will meet Allah Almighty while He is angry with him. What for one who abandons it? Paradise.""**"
"6904","Al-Urs ibn Umayrah رضي الله عنه reported that a man of Hadramawt and Imru al-Qays ibn Abis disputed concerning land and came to the Prophet ﷺ. He asked the Hadrami for proof, he had none, so he judged oath upon Imru al-Qays. The Hadrami said: O Messenger of Allah, if you grant him the oath, by Allah he will take my land. The Messenger ﷺ warned of meeting Allah angry for severing a Muslim's wealth by a lying oath, then called Imru al-Qays and recited to him: Indeed those who purchase with the covenant of Allah and their oaths a small price [Al Imran: 77]. He asked what for one who abandons it, was told Paradise, and testified he had abandoned it. > **""Whoever swears a lying oath to sever thereby the wealth of a Muslim will meet Allah while He is angry with him. Paradise.""**"
"6905","Iyad ibn Khalid said: I saw two men disputing before Maqil ibn Yasar رضي الله عنه, and Maqil reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning swearing to sever a man's wealth. > **""Whoever swears an oath to sever thereby the wealth of a man will meet Allah Blessed and Exalted while He is angry with him.""**"
"6906","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say concerning a servant or maidservant who swears a sinful oath at this minbar, even for a moist siwak. > **""There is no servant or maidservant who swears at this minbar a sinful oath, even for a moist siwak, except Hell becomes obligatory for him.""**"
"6907","Abu Salamah reported that Marwan said: Go and reconcile between these two, meaning Said ibn Zayd رضي الله عنه and Arwa bint Uways. They came to Said ibn Zayd, who said: Do you think I have diminished her right at all? I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mention the collar of seven earths for taking a span unjustly, the curse of Allah for taking alliance to a people without their permission, and absence of blessing for severing a Muslim's wealth by oath. > **""Whoever takes a span of land without its right will be collared with seven earths. And whoever takes alliance to a people without their permission, upon him is the curse of Allah. And whoever severs the wealth of a Muslim by an oath, Allah will not bless it for him.""**"
"6908","Abu Sud رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say concerning the wicked oath by which a man severs a Muslim's wealth. > **""The wicked oath by which a man severs the wealth of a Muslim sterilizes the womb.""**"
"6909","Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning the wicked oath. > **""The wicked oath removes wealth.""**"
"6910","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning one who swears an imposed oath while lying therein. > **""Whoever swears an imposed oath while he is lying therein, let him take his seat in Hell.""**"
"6911","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, repeating that the swiftest obedience in reward is joining kinship, that even a wicked household's wealth grows and number increases when they join kinship, and that the swiftest disobedience in punishment is tyranny and the submersive oath which removes wealth, burdens the womb and leaves homes desolate. > **""Indeed the swiftest obedience in reward is joining kinship. And the people of a household may be wicked, yet their wealth grows and their number increases when they join their kinship. And indeed the swiftest disobedience in punishment is tyranny and the submersive oath; it removes wealth and burdens the womb and leaves homes desolate.""**"
"6912","Salamah ibn al-Akwa رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said upon the minbar concerning swearing a lying oath. > **""None swears a lying oath except he takes his seat in Hell.""**"
"6913","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning swearing a lying oath to sever a Muslim's wealth. > **""Whoever swears a lying oath severing thereby the wealth of a Muslim will meet Allah on the Day of Resurrection while He is angry with him.""**"
"6914","Al-Ashath ibn Qays رضي الله عنه reported that Muadh was in dispute with a man, so judgment of oath fell upon one of them, whereupon the other said: O Messenger of Allah, will you leave him to swear and take it? The Prophet ﷺ spoke a stern word concerning lying in it. > **""If he swears lying.""**"
"6915","Al-Ashath ibn Qays رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say concerning swearing a binding oath to sever a Muslim's wealth. > **""Whoever swears a binding oath severing thereby the wealth of a Muslim will meet Allah Almighty while He is angry with him, whether He pardons him or punishes him.""**"
"6916","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that Allah Glorious in remembrance permitted him to narrate concerning a rooster whose feet have torn the earth while its neck is bent beneath the Throne, glorifying: Glory be to You, how great You are our Lord, to which is replied concerning one who swears falsely. > **""Indeed Allah, glorious in remembrance, permitted me to narrate concerning a rooster whose feet have torn the earth, while its neck is bent beneath the Throne, saying: Glory be to You, how great You are our Lord. So it is answered: That is not known by one who swears falsely by Me.""**"
"6917","Jabir ibn Atik رضي الله عنه reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say concerning severing a man's wealth by oath, asked even for something small, affirming even a siwak. > **""Whoever severs the wealth of a man by his oath, Allah forbids Paradise to him and makes Hell obligatory for him. Even if it is a siwak.""**"
"6918","Al-Harith ibn al-Barsa رضي الله عنه said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say while walking between the two jamrahs among the jamarat concerning taking anything of a Muslim's wealth by a wicked oath. > **""Whoever takes anything of the wealth of a Muslim by a wicked oath, let him take a house in Hell.""**"
"6919","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning swearing a deliberate lying oath. > **""Whoever swears a deliberate lying oath, let him take his seat in Hell.""**"
"6920","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه said: We used to count the submersive oath among the major sins."
"6921","Thalabah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning severing a right by a lying oath as a black dot of sirsal in his heart unaltered till Resurrection. > **""Any man who severs a right by a lying oath, it will be a black dot of sirsal in his heart which nothing will alter till the Day of Resurrection.""**"
"6922","Abu Ruhm al-Samai رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ mentioned the worst of thieves who steals the tongue of the ruler, the greatest sins of severing a Muslim's wealth without right, the goodness of visiting the sick with laying the hand and asking how he is, the best intercession of joining two in marriage till united, the trousers among the dress of former prophets, and supplication answered at sneezing. > **""Indeed among the worst of thieves is one who steals the tongue of the ruler. And indeed among the greatest sins is one who severs the wealth of a Muslim without right. And indeed among good deeds is visiting the sick, and of its completion is to place your hand upon him and ask how he is. And indeed among the best intercessions is to intercede between two concerning marriage till you join them. And indeed among the dress of former prophets is trousers, and indeed among what supplication is answered at is sneezing.""**"
"6923","Jubayr ibn Mutim رضي الله عنه ransomed his oath for ten thousand dirhams, then said: By the Lord of this Kabah, had I sworn I would have sworn truthfully; it is only something by which I ransomed my oath."
"6924","Al-Ashath ibn Qays رضي الله عنه said: I once purchased my oath for seventy thousand."
"6925","Abd al-Qahir ibn al-Sari said: A man hid with Abu al-Sawwar al-Adawi رحمه الله in the time of Hajjaj ibn Yusuf. It was said to Hajjaj: He is with Abu al-Sawwar. He sent for him and brought him, saying: The man is with you. He said: He is not with me. He said: Else Umm al-Sawwar is divorced, meaning the wife of Abu al-Sawwar. He said: I did not leave her intending her divorce. He said: Else you are free of Islam. He said: Then to where will you go? So he let him go."
"6926","Al-Shabi رحمه الله reported that al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad رضي الله عنه borrowed seven thousand dirhams from Uthman رضي الله عنه, and when he sought it he said: It is only four thousand. They disputed to Umar رضي الله عنه. Uthman said: I lent him seven thousand, and al-Miqdad said: Let him swear it is seven thousand. Uthman said: You have been just, but refused to swear, so Umar said: Take what he gives you. Then Uthman said: By Allah besides Whom there is no deity, it is seven thousand. Umar said: What prevented you from swearing that this is night and this is day."
"6927","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said thrice, each followed by exception. > **""By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, if Allah wills. By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, if Allah wills. By Allah, I shall surely raid Quraysh, if Allah wills.""**"
"6928","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما concerning His saying Almighty: And remember your Lord when you forget [Al-Kahf: 24], said it is exception, so make exception when you remember. He said: It is specific to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and none may make exception except in connection with the oath."
"6929","Abu Hurayrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning perfection of a servant's faith by making exception in every statement. > **""Indeed of the perfection of a servant's faith is to make exception in every statement.""**"
"6930","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, said: Whoever swears an oath then says: If Allah wills, he has made exception."
"6931","Aishah رضي الله عنها said: A woman gifted her dates in a dish, she ate some and some remained, so the woman said: I adjure you except you eat the rest. The Prophet ﷺ commanded her to fulfill it, for sin is upon the one who breaks. > **""Fulfil her oath, for sin is upon the one who breaks the oath.""**"
"6932","Abu Umamah al-Bahili رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ was with Aishah رضي الله عنها when her slave-girl brought qadid, saying: Eat this, my mistress, for its fragrance pleased me. She said: Keep it from me. She adjured her, yet she said: Keep it from me. The Prophet ﷺ warned of bearing its sin if she breaks her. > **""If you break her oath, its sin will be upon you.""**"
"6933","Abdullah, meaning Ibn Masud رضي الله عنه, said: We were commanded to fulfil oaths."
"6934","Abu Hazim رحمه الله reported that Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما passed a man with some sheep, asking for what price he would sell them, for such and such, whereupon he swore not to sell them. Ibn Umar went on, fulfilled his need, then passed him again, when the man said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, take them for what you offered me. He replied he had sworn an oath and he would not aid Shaytan against him nor cause him to break oath."
"6935","Abu Said al-Khudri رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning swearing then seeing better. > **""Whoever swears an oath then sees better than it, its expiation is to abandon it.""**"
"6936","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning swearing then seeing other, to do it as its expiation, except divorce or emancipation. > **""Whoever swears an oath then sees other than it, let him do it, for it is its expiation, except divorce or emancipation.""**"
"6937","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning swearing then seeing better. > **""Whoever swears an oath then sees other than it, meaning better than it, its expiation is to abandon it.""**"
"6938","Anas رضي الله عنه reported that Abu Musa رضي الله عنه sought a mount from the Prophet ﷺ, but met preoccupation, so he said: By Allah I will not carry you. When he turned away he called him and carried him. Abu Musa said: O Messenger of Allah, you swore not to carry me. He replied he now swears to carry him. > **""By Allah, I will not carry you. I swear I shall surely carry you.""**"
"6939","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه said: I came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking mounts for men of my people. He said twice: By Allah I will not carry you, by Allah I have nothing to carry you upon. Then the Prophet ﷺ was brought three fine-humped camels and sent for them and carried them. When they proceeded, I said to my companions: I do not see blessing in them, for the Messenger ﷺ swore not to carry us then carried us. They returned and informed him of his oath, and he replied he had not forgotten, but when he swears then sees better he does better and expiates. > **""I have not forgotten my oath, but when I swear an oath then see better than it, I do what is better and expiate my oath.""**"
"6940","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه also reported that Abu Musa رضي الله عنه came to the Prophet ﷺ seeking a mount, then mentioned the same long hadith."
"6941","Abu al-Darda رضي الله عنه said: Allah granted His Messenger ﷺ camels as fay and he distributed them. Abu Musa رضي الله عنه said: Grant me, O Messenger of Allah. He said: No. He repeated thrice, so the Prophet ﷺ said: By Allah I will not do it. Four fine-humped ones remained, so he said: Take them, O Abu Musa. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I had begged you and you refused and swore, so I feared delusion had entered upon the Messenger ﷺ. He replied that when he swears then sees better he expiates and does better. > **""When I swear then see other than that better, I expiate my oath and do what is better.""**"
"6942","Muawiyah ibn al-Hakam al-Sulami رضي الله عنه said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man who swears concerning a thing then regrets it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ replied concerning swearing then seeing better. > **""Whoever swears an oath then sees better than it, let him do what is better and expiate his oath.""**"
"6943","Abd al-Rahman ibn Udhaynah from his father رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning swearing then seeing better. > **""Whoever swears an oath then sees better than it, let him do what is better and expiate his oath.""**"
"6944","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning swearing then seeing better. > **""Whoever swears an oath then sees better than it, let him expiate his oath and do what is better.""**"
"6945","Umm Salamah رضي الله عنها swore concerning a slave of hers who sought manumission, saying: May Allah not free me from the Fire if I ever free him. She remained as long as Allah willed, then said: Glory be to Allah, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say concerning swearing then seeing better to expiate then do better. So she freed the slave then expiated her oath. > **""Whoever swears an oath then sees better than it, let him expiate his oath, then do what is better.""**"
"6946","Muawiyah ibn Haydah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed people competing in archery while swearing: You missed, by Allah; you hit, by Allah. When they saw the Messenger ﷺ they stopped, so he commanded them to shoot, for the oaths of archers are laghw without violation or expiation. > **""Shoot, for the oaths of archers are laghw; there is no violation therein, nor expiation.""**"
"6947","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seeking fatwa, having imposed upon himself a badanah in an oath he swore, so he gave him fatwa of a badanah of camels and rebuked the man against returning. > **""Slaughter a badanah of camels.""**"
"6948","Ibn Umar رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade vowing and commanded us to fulfil it. > **""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade vowing and commanded fulfilment of it.""**"
"6949","Kab ibn Ujrah رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent an expedition and said: If Allah keeps them safe I shall surely thank Him, or: Upon me if Allah keeps them safe to thank Him. They took booty and returned safe, so he said: O Allah, to You is praise as thanks and to You is favour as bounty. The people awaited him to do something, saw nothing, and reminded him of what he had said, so he asked had he not said it. > **""O Allah, to You is praise as thanks and to You is favour as bounty.""**"
"6950","Al-Hakam and Talhah ibn Musarrif رحمهما الله said: Maqil ibn Sinan رضي الله عنه came to Abdullah رضي الله عنه asking concerning a man who vows a vow without naming anything. He said: He frees a slave."
"6951","Jabir رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning a vow in disobedience of Allah Almighty. > **""There is no fulfilment of a vow in disobedience of Allah Almighty.""**"
"6952","A man reported that he performed Hajj tied to a relative of his. The Prophet ﷺ saw him and asked what this was. He said: It is a vow. So he commanded the tying to be cut. > **""What is this? Cut the tying.""**"
"6953","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ met two men tied together walking to the House. He asked what was the tying. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we vowed to walk to the House tied together. The Messenger ﷺ said it is not a vow, cut their tying, for a vow is only what seeks the Face of Allah Almighty. > **""What is the tying? This is not a vow; a vow is only what seeks thereby the Face of Allah Almighty.""**"
"6954","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed two men tied together performing Hajj by vow, and commanded them to remove their tying, saying then perform Hajj. > **""Remove your tying, then perform Hajj.""**"
"6955","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, on one of his journeys near Makkah, saw a woman with hair uncovered and asked who she was. They said: A woman of Quraysh who vowed to perform Hajj with hair uncovered, so he commanded her to veil. > **""Let her veil.""**"
"6956","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said concerning vowing only in obedience, none in severing kinship, and no divorce nor emancipation in what one does not own, with the addition in one narration of no oath in usurpation. > **""There is no vow except in what Allah Almighty is obeyed therein, and no vow in severing kinship, and no divorce nor emancipation in what one does not own. And no oath in usurpation.""**"
"6957","Ali ibn Abi Talib رضي الله عنه said: I have memorized for you six from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: No divorce except after marriage, no emancipation except after ownership, and no fulfilment of a vow in disobedience. > **""No divorce except after marriage, no emancipation except after ownership, and no fulfilment of a vow in disobedience.""**"
"6958","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنه reported that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed people on a severely hot day and saw a man standing as if a bedouin in the sun. He asked why he stood. He said: I vowed not to sit till you finish your khutbah. The Prophet ﷺ commanded him to sit, for this is not a vow; a vow is only what seeks the Face of Allah Almighty. > **""Sit; this is not a vow. A vow is only what seeks thereby the Face of Allah Almighty.""**"
"6959","Jabir رضي الله عنه said: Abu Isra'il رضي الله عنه vowed to stand a day in the sun till night without speaking, so the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to sit and speak. > **""Let him sit and speak.""**"
"6960","Al-Nawwas ibn Saman al-Kilabi رضي الله عنه said: The she-camel of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, al-Jada, was stolen, so he said: If Allah Almighty returns her to me I shall surely thank my Lord Almighty. She fell among a tribe of Arabs wherein was a Muslim woman. When camels pastured they pastured singly, and when they knelt they knelt singly placing their chins. The woman thought the camel familiar, and Allah cast into her mind to flee upon her. She found heedlessness, mounted, urged her, and arrived at Madinah by morning. The Muslims rejoiced and walked beside her till they came to the Messenger ﷺ. When he saw her he said: Praise be to Allah. The woman said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed if Allah saved me upon her to slaughter her and feed her meat to the poor. The Messenger ﷺ said she had ill rewarded her, with no vow except in what her right hand owns. They awaited whether he would initiate fast or prayer, thought he forgot, and reminded him of his vow to thank his Lord, so he asked had he not said praise be to Allah. > **""Praise be to Allah. Evil is what you rewarded her. There is no vow for you except in what your right hand owns. Did I not say: Praise be to Allah?""**"
"6961","Abdullah ibn Badr رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ said concerning vowing in disobedience. > **""There is no vow in disobedience.""**"
"6962","Abu Thalabah رضي الله عنه said: I came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed to slaughter camels of mine at an idol of Jahiliyyah? He said: Fulfil your vow, and do not sin against your Lord. Then the Messenger ﷺ said there is no fulfilment in disobedience, nor severing kinship, nor in what one does not own. > **""Fulfil your vow, and do not sin against your Lord. There is no fulfilment of a vow in disobedience, nor severing of kinship, nor in what one does not own.""**"
"6963","Kardam ibn Qays رضي الله عنه said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I vowed to slaughter camels of mine at such and such place. He said: Fulfil your vow; there is no vow in severing kinship, nor in what the son of Adam does not own. > **""Fulfil your vow. There is no vow in severing kinship, nor in what the son of Adam does not own.""**"
"6964","Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan رحمه الله reported that Safwan ibn al-Muattal رضي الله عنه vowed to strike Hassan ibn Thabit رضي الله عنه with the sword a blow."
"6965","Ali رضي الله عنه said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ saying: I vowed to slaughter my she-camel at such and such. He said: As for your she-camel, slaughter her; as for such and such, it is from Shaytan. > **""As for your she-camel, slaughter her; as for such and such, it is from Shaytan.""**"
"6966","Abu Isra'il رضي الله عنه reported that the Prophet ﷺ entered the mosque while Abu Isra'il prayed. It was said: Here he is, O Messenger of Allah; he does not sit, nor speak to people, nor seek shade, intending fasting. So the Messenger ﷺ commanded him to sit, speak, seek shade and fast. > **""Let him sit and speak to people and seek shade and fast.""**"
"6967","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that Uqbah ibn Amir رضي الله عنه came to the Prophet ﷺ mentioning his sister vowed to walk to the House. He commanded him to order her to ride and drive a badanah. > **""Order your sister to ride and drive a badanah.""**"
"6968","Aishah رضي الله عنها said: A man came to the Prophet ﷺ saying: My sister vowed to walk to the House. He commanded him to order her to ride, for Allah Almighty is free of her tormenting herself. > **""Order your sister to ride, for Allah Almighty is free of your sister tormenting herself.""**"
"6969","Imran ibn Husayn رضي الله عنه said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ never stood among us as khatib except he commanded charity and forbade muthlah, saying among muthlah is that a man vows to pierce his nose, and among muthlah is that a man vows to perform Hajj on foot, so let him drive a hady and ride. In another wording: Muthlah is that a man swears to perform Hajj tied or on foot, and whoever swears anything of that let him expiate his oath then ride. > **""Indeed of muthlah is that a man vows to pierce his nose; indeed of muthlah is that a man vows to perform Hajj on foot, so let him drive a hady and ride. Whoever swears anything of that, let him expiate his oath, then ride.""**"
"6970","Bishr رضي الله عنه embraced Islam, whereupon the Prophet ﷺ restored his wealth and his children to him. The Prophet ﷺ later met him and saw him with his son Talq bound together with a rope. He asked what this was, and Bishr replied that he had sworn that if Allah restored his wealth and children he would perform Hajj to the House of Allah bound. The Prophet ﷺ took the rope and cut it, and commanded the two to perform Hajj, explaining such self-torment is from Shaytan. > **""What is this, O Bishr? Perform Hajj, for this is from Shaytan.""**"
"6971","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that a man came with his mother to the Prophet ﷺ seeking to go out for jihad while his mother restrained him, so the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to remain with his mother, with like reward as jihad. Then another man came saying he had vowed to slaughter himself. The Prophet ﷺ became occupied, and the man departed with his mother and was found about to slaughter himself. The Prophet ﷺ praised Allah for placing in his Ummah one who fulfils the vow and fears a Day whose evil is widespread, asked him whether he possessed wealth, and when he said yes, commanded him to offer one hundred she-camels spread over three years, since none would take them from him all at once. > **""Remain with your mother, for you shall have with her the like of what you have in jihad. Praise be to Allah who has placed in my Ummah one who fulfils the vow and fears a Day whose evil shall be far-spreading. Do you possess wealth? Offer one hundred she-camels and spread it over three years, for you will not find any who will take them from you all at once.""**"
"6972","Ata ibn Abi Rabah رحمه الله reported that a man came to Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما and said he had vowed to slaughter himself. Ibn Abbas replied that in the Messenger of Allah ﷺ there is for them an excellent pattern of conduct, indicating such a vow is not to be carried out literally."
"6973","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما said in another narration that whoever vows to sacrifice himself or his child should slaughter a ram in its stead, thereby discharging the vow without unlawful bloodshed."
"6974","Masruq رحمه الله reported that Abdullah ibn Masud رضي الله عنه was brought an udder and began to eat from it, inviting the people to draw near. The people drew near while one man held back. Abdullah asked his affair, and he said he had made the udder unlawful upon himself. Abdullah described such self-imposed prohibition as from the whisperings of Shaytan, commanded him to draw near and eat and to expiate his oath, then recited: O you who believe, do not prohibit the good things which Allah has made lawful for you [Al-Maidah: 87]."
"6975","Abu al-Bakhtari رحمه الله reported that a dispute arose between a man from among the companions of Abdullah رضي الله عنه and his wife, after which she declared their condiment would only be from the milk of her goat, whereupon he swore he would eat nothing of its milk. When a guest came and she prepared food with that milk, the husband, wife, and guest each swore none would eat unless the others ate, so they passed the night without supper. When the account reached Abdullah and the man clarified there was no divorce nor zihar nor ila and narrated the affair, Abdullah adjured him that upon returning to his household the first thing he should do is eat from the milk of that goat, and viewed that he should satisfy himself by expiating his oath."
"6976","Khawwat ibn Jubayr رضي الله عنه reported that he fell ill and the Prophet ﷺ visited him. When he recovered, the Prophet ﷺ addressed him concerning his restored body and commanded him to fulfil for Allah what he had promised Him. When Khawwat replied he had promised Allah nothing, the Prophet ﷺ explained that no sick person falls ill except he vows something or intends some act of good, and commanded him to fulfil for Allah what he had promised. > **""Your body has recovered, O Khawwat, so fulfil for Allah what you promised Him. There is no sick person who falls ill except that he vows something or intends some act of good. So fulfil for Allah what you promised Him.""**"
"6977","Kardam ibn Sufyan رضي الله عنه asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning a vow he had taken in Jahiliyyah. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether it was for an idol or a sacrificial stone, and when he clarified it was for Allah Blessed and Exalted, the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to fulfil for Allah what he had assigned to Him, to slaughter seeking its reward and fulfil his vow. > **""Was it for an idol or for a sacrificial stone? Then fulfil for Allah, Blessed and Exalted is He, what you assigned to Him. Slaughter seeking its reward and fulfil your vow.""**"
"6978","The daughter of Kardamah reported from her father رضي الله عنه that he asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning his vow to slaughter three of his camels. The Prophet ﷺ distinguished that if it was upon a gathering of Jahiliyyah, or a festival of Jahiliyyah, or upon an idol, then it is void, and if upon other than that then he should discharge his vow. The man then asked whether he might walk on behalf of the mother of the girl who owed walking, to which the Prophet ﷺ gave permission. > **""If it was upon a gathering of the gatherings of Jahiliyyah, or upon a festival of the festivals of Jahiliyyah, or upon an idol, then no. And if it was upon other than that, then discharge your vow. Yes.""**"
"6979","Ibn Abbas رضي الله عنهما reported that Sad ibn Ubadah رضي الله عنه sought a verdict from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ concerning a vow incumbent upon his mother from Jahiliyyah, she having died before discharging it. The Prophet ﷺ commanded him to discharge it on her behalf. > **""Discharge it on her behalf.""**"
"6980","Sinan ibn Abdullah al-Juhani رضي الله عنه reported through his paternal aunt that she came to the Prophet ﷺ and said her mother had died owing a vow of walking to the Kaaba. The Prophet ﷺ asked whether she was able to walk on her behalf, and upon her affirmation commanded her to walk on behalf of her mother. When she asked whether that would suffice, he affirmed it by the parable of repaying a debt on behalf of the mother, declaring Allah more entitled to such settlement. > **""Are you able to walk on her behalf? Then walk on behalf of your mother. Yes. What think you: if your mother owed a debt and you then repaid it on her behalf, would it be accepted from you? Allah is more entitled to that.""**"
"6981","Marwan ibn Qays رضي الله عنه reported that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said his father had died having imposed upon himself to walk to Makkah and to sacrifice a badanah, leaving no wealth, asking whether it would be discharged if walked and sacrificed on his behalf from the son's own wealth. The Prophet ﷺ answered affirmatively and commanded him to discharge, sacrifice, and walk on his behalf, illustrating it with the parable of a debt owed to a man and Allah's greater right to be pleased. > **""Yes, discharge it on his behalf, and sacrifice on his behalf and walk on his behalf. What think you: if your father owed a debt to a man and you repaid it on his behalf from your wealth, would not the man return satisfied? Allah Exalted is He is more entitled to be pleased.""**"
"6982","Abdullah ibn Amr رضي الله عنهما reported that al-As ibn Wail had vowed in Jahiliyyah to slaughter one hundred budn, and Hisham ibn al-As had slaughtered his fifty share. When Amr رضي الله عنه asked the Prophet ﷺ concerning the remainder, the Prophet ﷺ made benefit conditional upon the father's affirmation of Tawhid, that fasting and charity on his behalf would then benefit him. > **""As for your father, had he affirmed Tawhid, then had you fasted and given charity on his behalf, that would have benefited him.""**"
"6983","Ata ibn Abi Rabah رحمه الله reported that al-Sharid رضي الله عنه came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on the day of the Conquest and said he had vowed that if Allah Mighty and Majestic granted him the conquest of Makkah he would pray in Bayt al-Maqdis. The Prophet ﷺ repeatedly directed him to pray in his present place. > **""Here, then pray. Here, then pray. Here, then pray.""**"
